  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe main concern in the short term is the potential for heavy \nrainfall late this afternoon through tomorrow morning potentially \nleading to flash and river flooding. In addition there will be the \nchance for a few strong storms late this afternoon through this \nevening that will be capable of damaging winds and large hail. The \nfocus for these storms and the heavy rainfall will be a slow moving \ncold frontal boundary. This boundary is slowly sagging into the \narea, and is currently extending from Unionville to St Joseph early \nthis afternoon. This front is expected to continue to slowly sink \nsouth through the afternoon. Light showers and cloud cover had \npersisted north of this boundary creating an area of differential \nheating where temperatures have achieved the low to mid 90s ahead of \nthe boundary and 70s north. The environment ahead of the front is \nmoderately unstable with MUCAPE values in the 2000-3000J/Kg range \nhowever, the stronger effective shear lags behind the boundary. As \nsuch, the main threat with strongest storms that develop late this \nafternoon into the evening will be capable of localized damaging \nwinds and brief small hail. What is the biggest concern however, is \nthe threat for heavy rainfall that trains along and north of the \nboundary this evening through the overnight. PWATs are near record \nvalues Betteann 2.20\"-2.40\" so storms will be extremely efficient \nwith rainfall totals of 2-4\" forecast for portions of northeastern \nKansas and NW Missouri. This will lead to a flood threat \nparticularly for areas of NE KS/NW MO that received 4-7\" of rain \nyesterday morning. As such a flood watch is in effect for these \nareas through early tomorrow afternoon.\n\nStorms are expected to continue through the overnight tonight fueled \nby a southwesterly 30-35kt LLJ nosing into the area. The LLJ is \nprogged to weaken tomorrow morning allowing showers to dissipate. \nTomorrow afternoon into tomorrow morning the frontal boundary will \nlift back north across the CWA as a warm front. Showers and \nthunderstorms are expected with this feature as it lifts north with \nthe best chances for storms (25-35%) along and north of Highway 36 \ncorridor. Highs tomorrow will range from the low to mid 90s south of \nthe Missouri River, where the warm front will pass earlier in the \nday, to the mid 70s across northern Missouri where the warm front \nwon't lift through until tomorrow night. Thursday into Thursday \nnight looks to remain mostly dry and warm with the war front north \nof the area. Highs will range from the mid 80s to mid 90s. Heat \nindex values south of the Missouri River may approach 105 degrees \nwith a heat advisory possibly being necessary.\n\nThe upper-level ridge will shift slightly back toward the south-\ncentral US, drying things out briefly Friday morning and lifting \nhigh temperatures into the 90s. However, this will be short-lived, \nas an upper-level trough and surface cold front move into our region \nFriday night into Saturday, bringing rain along the front. PWAT \nvalues from the GFS range from 1.5-2.0 inches for Saturday and \nSunday, which fall in the 90th percentile for climatology. This, on \ntop of the rain we could see earlier in the week, may increase the \nrisk of flooding, especially in NW MO. By Monday, the cold front \nmoves out and high pressure moves in bringing cooler and drier \nweather with highs in the 70s.",
